    "seconds": 29,
    "summary": "The `rate` service was crash-looping, and it's now running and Ready. All 19 pods in `test-hotel-reservation` are Running and Ready.\n\n**What I found**\n- The `rate` pod exited on startup with `not authorized on rate-db`.\n- The `admin` user on `mongodb-rate` had lost its `readWrite` role on `rate-db`. It only kept `userAdminAnyDatabase`. The `root` user still had its grant.\n- The namespace has a `failure-admin-rate` ConfigMap containing a `revoke-admin-rate-mongo.sh` script that removes exactly that grant, and a matching restore script. I found no Job or CronJob that would re-run the revoke, so the fault was a one-time change.\n\n**What I changed**\n- Granted `admin` the `readWrite` role on `rate-db` on `mongodb-rate`, using the same command as the restore script, run as `root`. This restores the role the service depends on and is not a new privilege.\n- Deleted the crash-looping `rate` pod so the replacement started fresh. The new pod connected to the database, registered with Consul, and logged no authorization errors.\n\n**Not done**\n- I didn't change the ConfigMaps or the MongoDB deployment. The revoke script and the `failure-admin-rate` ConfigMap are still there, so if something re-runs the revoke, the outage will come back. I'd suggest finding out what applied it.\n- The `admin` and `root` passwords are still the defaults (`admin`/`root`) in the ConfigMaps. That's a separate security issue.",
    "timed_out": false,
